 The barrier already gave this distinction consequences. Its visible plank and its invisible collision shape occupied different amounts of space. Here the thin rods have slightly wider pickup shapes so a hand can find them. What we can see and what we can touch are being decided together, but they need not coincide.
 
+Two long lines run along the floor. They keep the same distance apart, but from the entrance they seem to draw together. The intervals marked along them are equal too. The ones further away look smaller. A pair of lines can suggest depth in a flat drawing. Here you can walk into the depth they describe.
+
+Look up. A dark line follows the edge of a narrow strip of ceiling. From the small mark near the entrance, a second line seems to belong to that edge. Step sideways. It comes away from the ceiling: a length suspended nearer to you, waiting at the same height all along. Nothing moved except your view.
+
+For a moment, the room let two separate things become one line. It took your position to make them agree. Another step gives them back their distance.
+
 ## Four views
 
 Two sticks make a plus. Further along, two of the same length make an X. From the marked starting position, each reads as one familiar sign. Step sideways. The sticks sit in slightly different depth planes; the crossing belongs to your view. Take one in your hand and bring it forward. How far can the sign come apart before you stop seeing it?
